    class Box {
        int length;

        Box(int length) {
            this.length = length;
        }
    }
🔹 Explanation:
this is a reference variable in Java that refers to the current object (i.e., the object being created).
In the constructor, both the parameter and the instance variable have the same name (length).
To differentiate between them, we use this.length to refer to the instance variable, and length alone refers to the constructor parameter.

🔹 Without this:
length = length; // This assigns the parameter to itself, not the instance variable.

🔹If there's no name conflict, this can be omitted(avoided):
    Box(int l) {
        length = l; // No conflict, so `this` is optional.
    }

