Student st = new OfficeEmployee();
🧠 What’s Happening:
Student → Parent class (Base type / reference)
OfficeEmployee → Child class (extends Student)
st → Reference variable of type Student
new OfficeEmployee() → Object created of type OfficeEmployee

📌 Real-Life Connection:
“Main ek student hoon, maine office me trainee ke roop me join kiya.
Par jab tak mujhe koi actual kaam assign nahi hota,
main sirf student-type kaam hi kar sakti hoon.”

📊 Java Behavior:
Student st = new OfficeEmployee();	Tum office join kar chuki ho, but treated as a student (limited role)
st.study();	Allowed — Tum abhi sirf training le rahi ho
st.doOfficeWork();	❌ Error — Office ka kaam tumhe assign nahi hua, tumhe student mana gaya
((OfficeEmployee) st).doOfficeWork();	✅ Tumne prove kar diya tum OfficeEmployee ho — ab kaam milega

🔁 Full Access Example:
OfficeEmployee oe = new OfficeEmployee();
oe.study();         // ✅ Allowed
oe.doOfficeWork();  // ✅ Allowed
Agar tumhe tumhari asli identity (OfficeEmployee) se bulaya jaaye, toh tumhe full access milta hai.

🔚 Final Summary:
"Object ke paas jitni bhi qualities hoon,
tum usse reference me jis role me treat karoge,
wohi kaam karwa paoge —
unless you downcast and accept the full identity."


🔹Box b = new BoxW(); means:

A BoxW-type object is created in memory (actual object)
A reference of type Box (b) is used to point to it
Access is limited to Box's visible methods, unless methods are overridden


🔹Box b = new BoxW(); — but what's the benefit of creating a BoxW object when we can't even use its features?
Why not just write Box b = new Box(); directly?

The object is future-ready. If I’m handling the object using a Box reference,
I can still access its full capabilities in the future through downcasting —
without rewriting the existing code. This approach is highly useful in building scalable applications.

